        "text": "<|endoftext|> plaintiffs have already filed. What should concern a rider in mind is not whether these services would pass the \"Consumer Privacy Law\" test, but what they at least do.\n\n'Decades of misguided legislation and dramatically slow enforcement'\n\nThe Privacy Act establishes guidelines for how information used by third parties such as ISPs, social networks, and phone service providers should be collected, displayed, transmitted or archived. The bill similarly sets out security standards for the collection and use of far more sensitive data held by them than what is publicly available. (Connecticut is the only state in the nation that requires data retention for both personal data and business records.)\n\nBut the Protect IP Act aims to expand those security requirements so that they include not just consumers' IP addresses but also their phone numbers. The intent is that in future cases when a broadband provider leaves any number of calls or SMS messages available on the network to a third-party agency like Verizon, Internet service providers will have the authority under the law to retain those internal call history records in its custody. (More specifically, it has been argued that there's no way in particular that telecoms and their contractors could possibly retain such such information for longer than two hours after a legal technician leaves work.)\n\nThe legislation also bans academics or researchers from assisting against the nation's broadband providers in othology applications, the law's basis for the regulation.\n\nThe short version is that technology companies could not show benefit from Freedom of Information Act (FOIA) requests filed with the FCC or any other agency to obtain aggregate amounts of information nor prevent their consultants from seeking consent first for applications. The law does allow for whistleblowing, but only in cases of long-term government retaliation, but it makes it off the list of things that essentially won't kill a bill. While the FCC's authority over telecoms may be limited, it does not mean that it'll merely continue to be so. In fact, the legislation cites the \"Top Eight co-sponsors\" of the bill as having made the first critical decisions in crafting the legislation.\n\nMore to the point, this is an exception in a larger trend of massive digital security solutions, such as the recently acquired capabilities of Google's G Suite software, meaning that both the legality and technical merit of backing an all-out law against third-party enforcement is high to start with. If Congress keeps on supporting these programs thus far, however, the prospects for these bills will only get steeper and the standing of these policies will wane.\n\nThe Protection IP Act is certainly progress toward what the FCC intends to do, but it's still straying far enough from the intentions of Congress in the first place. It erases basic privacy protections, provides no guidance on how to use the data, and reinforces the Patriot Act as appropriate in controversial areas such as expanded spy agencies. In the meantime, people should be hopeful that once this controversial set of bills is in place, the law will have little to do with the fight against digital privacy.<|endoftext|>In its latest “critical outpost” report on Gaza, the United Nations’ own human rights body slams Israel on “despicable and unlawful violations of basic rights, including access for humanitarian purposes to, and protection of, civilian populations, access by high-level civilians to humanitarian aid supplies, and security at all sites.”\n\nA new report from the Dubai-based think tank JIC finds that Israel is the world’s fifth worst violator of human rights. It decries what it calls “judicial and legislative house-rules that routinely allow the use of arbitrary and unlawful arrest and detention to coerce individuals into divulging their whims or behavior,” as well as the “record of arbitrary and unjust detention of persons indefinitely detained under political, economic, economic, social or other constraints,” as well as the denial of basic physical or mental rights directly to Palestinians.\n\nWith Arab Spring-inspired revolts in Egypt and Yemen sweeping across the Arab world, including in countries such as Bahrain and Egypt, the UN presented itself as a legitimate voice advocating for human rights on the ground in the occupied Palestinian territories. Despite months of unrest, resolutions, and acts of resilience, key issues remain to be addressed, the report found, including the imprisonment of many people who use weapons against Hamas, the disruption of the Gaza-to-Israel (HTC):\n\nPockets of armed resistance remain a major hurdle to the achievement of Gazan government accountability and civilian security. Notwithstanding efforts by Israel, Hamas, and similar actors to negotiate a framework for meeting the Gaza-to-Israel (HTC) demand, the level of criminality remains high and represents an alarming preoccupation of Palestinian authorities, under Israeli/American control, with resuming the indiscriminate indiscriminate killing of civilians. A momentum towards creating an appropriate international framework for direct rule has been evident from the beginning. In the twelve months to September 2016, the UN Commission of Inquiry for Gaza found no evidence of violations of"
