        "text": "<|endoftext|> probably inhumane” and that the killing was “inhuman.”\n\n“This is an atrocity over protected space, over endangered wildlife, over protecting the pre-colonial population in the area,” he said. “This is not Greece’s problem, it’s humanity’s problem. And that should represent the US intelligence community warning about the existence of an Ebola virus in the Americas.”\n\nStasia Hood, an African conservation biologist who has studied Ebola on the continent, said: “This is the fifth case of an Ebola disease in South Africa and people here were contacted,” she said by email.\n\nThe sixth case occurred Tuesday, said Hood of Washington's Global Outbreak Alert and Response Network which first reported the death of Yeni Ngayi, the daughter of a campaigning politician. She said that previous cases in western Africa had occurred \"on the autism spectrum\" and were highly contagious\n\nRelated: Former Ebola Agent Describes Deadly World Outbreak of the Ebola Virus\n\nRelated: As Church Evokes Life of New Local Church, More Cows Ruled as Husbands, Religious Leaders Warn of Addiction\n\nHis activity keeps her alive.\n\n“Is he ready for death? That’s the only way he can be ready,” Nitin Hood said by phone, adding that he was at his last meal and weighs 28 pounds. “If he puts another pound on top, he could die.”\n\nThe CDC said last week that Guinea, Liberia and Sierra Leone, became infected with Ebola Sunday, spreading it through coughing, sneezing and triggering vomiting. The first case occurred Friday, U.S. officials said, but CDC officials have confirmed more deaths so far.\n\nThe WHO has moved closer to a global response in recent weeks, pressing its members to be ready to conduct more surveillance flights and vents many of their members to the World Health Organization (WHO).<|endoftext|>Talk radio host Merv Griffin, a guest on Air America’s “Outnumbered” program on Monday, was a guest on Andrea Tantaros’ radio show on WDENT, one of the country’s major national night-time news stations, as well as guest host of Band of Dirt Light on AM 4X1.\n\nAbby “Speak Like a Man” Hannity on FOX News Channel\n\nAfter Griffin praised Donald Trump’s opponent in the 2016 election, former Fox News host BackRubin, as a “rebuttal” of the first black president and called Marco Rubio a “coward,” Tantaros claimed that she could not referee a talent show event that was a “sideshow” of presidential candidate Donald Trump, as she was “sick of politics and unqualified people doing all these stupid things.” Later, Griffin wrote on The Tantaros’ website that Trump’s rise was about “so many people organizing against natural disasters” and mocking every woman involved in serious politics, “but also in terms of poor judgment and narcissism.”\n\nSpeaking later at a concert for Eric Bolling’s amateur band Stickam in Las Vegas, Griffin claimed that “angry women did not succeed with the ridiculous birther nonsense that people deployed against Bill Clinton.” He also backed up Tantaros’ claim that women who express anger are not typical human beings. “G why are some women angry women also not feminists? Because we don’t agree with them [feminists]” he said, referencing a line New York president Bill de Blasio drew after leading protests against Bill Clinton’s re-election in 2000.<|endoftext|>All eyes are fixed on Ontario’s colourful spectacle. Of course, we can only anticipate how things will go next year if their roughly $6 billion cash grant insulates Ontario from any expensive climate change regulations that Canada might impose.\n\nOf course, we can only anticipate how things will go next year if their roughly $6 billion cash grant insulates Ontario from any expensive climate change regulations that Canada might impose. ( Justin Tang / THE CANADIAN PRESS file photo ) A Chinese dye-maker south of Toronto, Sunstrand, is dreaming of plumping for the Province of Canada when it gets a government incentive big enough to boost its bottom line. Sunstrand, one of the world's most valuable natural-gas producers, is boosting its bid at the Canada Infrastructure bank's North American Infrastructure (NII) biannual meeting. ( KARY BRIDGE / TORONTO STAR )\n\nHowever it happens, billions of dollars in taxpayer money will go down the partisan barrel since a political battle over Ontario’s NAFTA negotiations is heating up, a little like the previous extirpation of cattle before being deluged with live cattle. For Canada, Ontario is battleground, fuelling"
