        "text": "<|endoftext|> which prompted the Near East Office to clarify its position following Baloch's arrest.\n\nBaloch is also one of the more prominent jihadists fighting in Afghanistan and its Hisat Province. He was reportedly instrumental in sparking the retreat of Muammar Qaddafi's forces last year. Baloch and Hizb-ut-Tahrir founder Rizwan Ahmadi have also fought against Qadaffi's regime, which believes Baloch originated his own version of Islam in Vancouver, Western Canada and Canada, and the LTTE in Tamil Nadu.\n\nHe has sustained heavy ridicule on social media. Over 4,000 people have reposted his links to the LTTE. A key Twitter account created by him in June 2013 tweeted: \"Remember #qanthetraj. There man drowns in memes :)\"<|endoftext|>Tommy Smith (6/14/1987 – Sunday, April 14, 2015) was a Canadian football player for the Philadelphia Eagles of the National Football League.\n\nProfessional career [ edit ]\n\nWith the Eagles (2002–2007), Smith began his playing career and played in all 16 games as an offensive back for the Philadelphia Eagles. Following his position change, Smith accumulated 286 receptions, 804 receiving yards (8.4) and two touchdowns. San Francisco also sent down its only draft pick to the Eagles: Akron guard Keith Bogansworth.[1][2][3]\n\nOn September 24, 2008, after a 14-year pro career, Smith joined the Eagles following the 2007 season. Smith sat out the 2008 season with an oblique injury after suffering a knee injury in the second preseason game against the New York Giants. Smith struggled in his fourth season and in the 2009 season was placed on the year-ending injured reserve list.[4][5][6] When the Eagles announced their Pro Bowl selection of Mark Smith in October 2009, he was placed on team reserve while recovering from the injury.[7] On December 12, 2010, Smith signed with the Indianapolis Colts on a one-year contract.[8][9] He spent the 2011 season on injured reserve.[11]\n\nDuring his first two seasons with the Eagles, Smith suffered nine concussions, four of which were for head injury related injuries. He was fined $1,200 in 2012 and fined $100,000 a third time after testing negative for neck and head injuries in Indianapolis. The Philadelphia Eagles' policy prohibiting fines on concussions is updated each year. In December 2013, on season day 10, Smith was suspended one game.[12][13][14]\n\nAfter the 2013 season, the Eagles selected running back Eddie Lacy in the first round of the 2014 NFL Draft.[15] He was placed on the practice squad early in the 2015 season on a conditioning program to run faster than he typically does and He finished with just 81 yards on 226 attempts with only one touchdown.<|endoftext|>Story highlights Trump drew the biggest number of protesters among super PAC donors, according to the data\n\nNatter said those were the donations that encompass super PAC donations to elect Sen. Tom Cotton\n\nWashington (CNN) After the election, Arkansas Sen. Tom Cotton has pulled in 19 million more dollars than his opponent, Sen. Susan Collins, gives back.\n\nThe Democratic Susan Collins, who has since announced she will not seek reelection, outspent Trump's by a sixth, even after buying into the ad campaign from his super PAC supporting Cotton and friendly donors, The Hill reported.\n\nThe Trump super PAC, the Donald J. Trump Foundation, gave nearly seven million dollars to the advertising effort.\n\nThat money -- and Cotton's decision to distance himself from Trump's enterprise in the wake of President Donald Trump's win -- has won the kind of buy-in from super PACs, and helped him pull in more money than most new Democratic senators.\n\n\"I think he was wounded by what we've seen in the first days after the election,\" Trump Foundation president David Sklansky told that Huffington Post. \"I think at this point it's clear to a lot of people that voter turnout was far worse than people thought they were going to get. And people were very, very disappointed.\"\n\nRead More<|endoftext|>Titan Crossing the Universe with the Carroff SLS rocket, 8.725 km in with ICE, providing crew communications and power. Photo Credit: NASA / ESA / Phil Stooke A view of NASA's LDC Fairfield with 60 million light-years of 'processing power' aboard the upper stage engine. The LDC Fairfield used the second engine with 34 Gbps of integrated circuits (IC). The experimental Light Heavy Rocket A Camera System (LHASH), ran a 28 (cradle-to-plane) camera system about 8 feet in length (4 meters) with hexagonal 6 inches (140 mm) by 120 inches (3,344 mm × 262 mm) siding. The two NOAA satellites ESA-Earth (ESS) and U.S. Geological Survey satellite KIOP (K"
