        "id": "s-0c2630cc8a114dfd8fc1f56e",
        "sourceId": 159,
        "text": "The Dolphins also made a comeback against the Carolina Vikings for Jerick Bell, who threw for a 25-yard touchdown on Nov. 5 and Lamar Jones, an 80-yard field goal, on Nov. 6. \"The most difficult time he's ever had is how destructive the injury is in terms of choosing an approach,\" Burry said. \"I don't like to think that he's accustomed to it. Maybe that's something he suffers from. But right now he think he is choosing the right approach.\" Watch video COUELLS, Ohio (CNN) -- Saints quarterback Craig Peters has returned to regular preseason action with an hamstring injury and remains confident he'll be able to come back to full capacity if the season doesn't come to pass. \"There are a lot of tough spots on my shoulders right now. I'll be working with them and doing everything I can to stay healthy,\" Peters said. \"Hopefully, the shoulders surgery and the psychoscopy in my knee are going to be good. I'm focused on getting back to full capacity and staying healthy going into the season.\" The injury will be a setback for Peters, who will undergo surgery on Sunday, Dec. 5, to bring him back to full capacity. As the team heads into the preseason for the first time, head coach Mario Marinotiseta was asked about the injury. \"The specialists have been working on it for the last couple days and I'm excited to get back from it. Also, as far as the season goes, I'm hopeful that I can cope with it,\" Peters said from CoX Medical Center. \"It'll definitely be nice to get my shoulder injury up again. I'm excited to be able to cope with it. That is what I'm after.\" Peters will begin practice for the Saints on the first play of the regular preseason opener, Sunday (a Dec. 6 loss to the Titans at CoX Arena). The 38-year-old Peters is one of only three of the quarterbacks named as the CFL Player of the Week. In total, Peters threw over 35 yards from the ground with 17.67 yards per attempt on 46.2 percent of his plays coming from the ground. On the final possession against Charlotte, while playing with his Panthers room-mates, Peters drove up the 25-yard line at the Charlotte bench, taking a 31-38 lead with 3:31 seconds left before the clock. After Peters slammed Panthers defensive end Mike Durant and sunk an 80-yard run off the Saints bench, Peters threw a season-high 57-yard field goal for a touchdown. \"He did really well. The practice from the first week was really good,\" Burry said. \"He started the week he learned a little bit and when he recovers he'll do really, really well on the ground. \"The reason he's coming back really well and I think he's improved is he's been in great recovery. He's made a lot of great kickbacks, he's done a really good job of himself and when he comes back we look forward to practice as soon as we can.\" There was also positive news for Peters, who already underwent hiptherapy in May and signed onto the practice squad in June. \"He's really excited to have a great season here in Ohio and a solid recovery moving forward,\" Marinotiseta said. \"I'm hopeful that his progress starts to make at the end of the year as he starts staying healthy, starts dealing with the stress and becomes ready to return to full capacity minus injury.\" Peters has been one of the best running backs to have ever played in the NFL Games. The Saints gave him 20 rushing yards and touchdowns last season; first in the league in rushing yards per attempt (10). A teen accused of planning to shoot and kill a police officer on sheriff's duties has been sentenced to 4 years behind bars over the incident. The Providence County Sheriff's Office said about 11 p.m. that they found a 13-year-old teen inside his car and he was planning to shoot and kill someone in a sheriff's van in Applewood Ridge. Sylvia Grant, 64 (510 S. 52rd St), who was arrested on five counts of aggravated use of a deadly weapon, falsely planning to point a gun at a Providence police officer, and lying to commit homicide, was sentenced to 1 year behind bars. For current updates about Providence, please click HERE"
