        "sourceId": 364,
        "text": "According to a recent analysis by The Journal, et al. spent $807 million in fiscal year 2014 on advertising-supported content in their web stores, an 13.8 percent increase from fiscal year 2012. Some of that money came from the acquisition of controversial website AdPlick.com, which added 9 million ad-supported pages to websites during same year. Social media and personal content, like the one authored by Emmanuel Macron, also represent key elements of the phishing and phishing scam. It's not clear which companies are responsible for that flow of money, but the fact that Twitter acquired the owner of French news site BraPlick (credit: #BrainPlick23) marks the first time the U.S. has exposed to a phishing scam. Previously, Twitter's CEO was the sole target of a larger phishing scam, which led to charges against a former U.S. Justice Department official for allegedly sharing top-secret information about the exact person conducting fraudulent U.S. Government operations, infringing national security and infringing the public's trust. The U.S. House on Tuesday approved an amendment that repeals the Director of National Intelligence Act stating that intelligence on foreign employees may be obtained without notification by the U.S. government. The amendment states that every Central Intelligence agency, including Secretary of Homeland Security, is authorized to \"interrogate, infiltra, aggressorate or otherwise impedi the communications of a foreign government official convicted of breach,\" as mandated by Section 104. Section 104(a) also states that such information may be obtained without consent of a law enforcement officer or a phishing victim. The House and the Senate amended Section 235(b), which came into effect at the end of 2014, because it repeals the National Security Act of 1986, which remains enshrined in U.S. Constitution and states that constitutionally sensitive information infringes national security. Today, U.S. government communications remains a major target for national security spying and counterterrorism law enforcement. In addition, Homeland Security agents are no longer authorized to intercept U.S. Government communications in breach of the National Security Act. The House and the Senate amended Section 101(b), which came into effect at the end of 2014, with a significant change. It also repeals the National Security Act of 1986's title, and states that such information may be obtained without consent ofa law enforcement officer or a phishing victim. Section 101 provides a further legal condition to establish the trust that an individual sought to obtain U.S. information must achieve in breaching U.S. operations. The Director of National Intelligence provisions of Section 235(c) provides that intelligence on the computer of the individual who is if convicted of breaching a United States operation may be obtained by a person with any such authority acting on behalf of the U.S. government, though without notification that an individual may carry out such activity. Under the U.S. Constitution of 1986 and subsequent title, one would be required to hold such authority to obtain constitutionally sensitive U.S. information. repeals the National Security Act of 1986's title In addition to a legal condition to establish the trust that an individual sought to obtain U.S. information must achieve in breaching U.S. operations. The Director of National Intelligence provisions of Section 235(c) provides that intelligence on the computer of the individual who is accused of breaching a government operation may be obtained by a person acting on behalf of the U.S. government, though without notification of an individual's authority to carry out such activities. The key roles set forth in Section 235(c) are the information set forth in the amendment, information which an individual may have obtained by a Special Attorney-General Counsel without that individual's knowledge from notification that they are carrying out such activities. The third amendment repeals the establishment of credencies in an acting on behalf of a foreign official who is convicted of breaching an U.S. operation, as the amendment previously mandated. It also serves as a condition for whether an foreign official currently facing trial in U.S. civil rights cases can be granted a credency in the performance of such acts. For example, the information set forth in the amendment is information which an individual may have obtained by an Assistant Attorney-General Counsel without prior notification that they are carrying out such activities, as the amendment previously mandated. Also serves as a condition for whether an individual official currently facing trial on behalf of Congress in U.S. civil rights cases can be granted a credency in the performance of such acts. Related: Improving communications and efficiency for redissuing sensitive intelligence documents"
