        "sourceId": 833,
        "text": "Rep. Rand Paul (R-Ky.), the top House Majority Leader, also refused to back Trump's plan to repeal Medicaid. In a Tuesday interview with \"Face the Nation,\" Paul stated that although Trump initially announced his proposal, it now has to be done by the Democratic Party. \"It was better to work with the Democratic Party than work with the Republican Party,\" Paul, Kentucky Republican, told Buzzfeed News. \"It has to be done by the Republican Party. I believe that we'll be able to repeal President Obamacare.\" Senate Majority Leader Tom Graham Jr. (D-Nebi.) said he supports the Republican leadership's efforts to move to a complete repeal of Obamacare. \"I absolutely support the Republican leadership to repeal its policy of helping millions of uninsured Americans buy insurance,\" Graham said on CNN. \"But before we get to that, we need to repeal.\" Graham, a neocontant who has been working on the revised law, said he has already reached out to some Republican lawmakers who have been blocking Trump's move. \"If we can still do it of course not,\" Graham told CNN. \"At the same time, if there is support from the Republican Party or House Democrats, then we're going to be their first choice.\" As the Senate looks to pass a bill to repeal the Affordable Care Act by defunding health care altogether that would help millions more Americans buy insurance, Republicans are aiming to move toward a complete repeal of Obamacare in order to push forward on a replacement bill. Currently, Republicans have been working on a controversial plan that would prevent certain individuals, groups of businesses, or insurance companies from increasing Americans' health deductibles. Republicans have largely been lobbying with Democrats for such legislation at the House's Republican Caucus, and are expected to introduce the bill to the Senate. But Republicans are wary — that repeal of Obamacare isn't going to happen. \"It's hard to do,\" said Rep. John Duran (R-N.H.), the top Democrat on the House Energy and Commerce Committee, who said he is backing the proposal. \"But if we're in a debacle, then we're going to lose the law.\" Republican lawmakers have expressed concern that Trump's move would block progress on an repealing bill that is currently pushing through the Senate. \"A bill to repeal the Affordable Care Act would replace the Affordable Care Act by defunding health care altogether, put a significant burden on government funding and ensure millions of uninsured Americans are able to afford health care by 2020,\" CNN political analyst Richard Chozsky said. According to the latest NBC News/Wall Street Journal/ABC/Marist poll, 52 percent of voters backing Trump said Republicans would move to complete repeal of Obamacare. Many of those voters are likely to vote on a unanimous measure that would make the law permanent by the time Trump takes office. So one of the main questions before Trump assumes the presidency is whether or not Republicans should vote to repeal Medicaid. Medicaid has long been the focus for an Obamacare repeal, and Trump announced in a tweet on Tuesday that he expects to put the plan on the polls next week. Earlier this week, Trump insisted that his repeal initiative wouldn't be passed through the Senate until he announced his plan to replace the Affordable Care Act to allow more Americans to buy health insurance online. After Trump takes office, Republicans will try to move toward a complete repeal of Obamacare by 2018, but are in no position to vote on a unanimous measure to repeal the law. Republicans are considering a proposal by Trump to move to a complete repeal of Obamacare in order to push forward on a replacement bill to repeal it. Senate Majority Leader Dianne Feinstein (D-Mass.) and Paul (R-Utah) say they are also unlikely to support a bill to repeal unless they support Trump's proposal that would allow \"millions of uninsured Americans to buy health insurance.\" And Senate Majority Leader Mitch McGovern (D-W.Va.) told The Washington Post on Tuesday that he has no support for Trump's plan to replace Medicaid. In February, the Senate backed a House initiative that expanded the eligibility period for federal and state subsid programs. Duran said the measure puts a significant burden on private sector funding to cover millions of uninsured Americans and that more work was needed to ensure that more uninsured Americans were able to afford insurance by 2020. \"The Republican leadership is trying to repeal its policy of letting millions of uninsured Americans buy health insurance,\" Duran said.? because? let us summarize: how to do it"
