        "sourceId": 866,
        "text": "As part a decades-long study, the researchers examined millions of acres of groundwater destroyed by storms, fires and snowfall near and around the Mississippi River in sub-Southeastern Mississippi, as well as West Worth and San Joseph counties from 1996 to 2010, according to a signed affidavit. Sen. Charles Whittenmore endorsed the team, D-Wash, while James C. Arault, an oceans scientist at the Charles Thohart Institute for Oceanographic Research, said insufficient groundwater in the lakes plays a role in curbing large-scale pollution. \"The frequent poisoning of groundwater is also a significant factor in slowing down water pollution in these areas,\" Arault said. In the Great Lake state of Iowa, 90 percent of the water that can be tapped goes to the source of soaring illnesses like malaria. The federal government has issued an executive order in 10 states, including Iowa, forcing groundwater drilling near lakes with limited population or infrastructure, releasing waste water into the lakes and, in some cases, banning companies who drilled them. Tom Moreman says he died of a heart attack. (Photo11: The Washington Post)Buy Photo Story Tom Moreman says he died of a heart attack, which occurred seven years ago. Moreman was only 72 at the time, but he was living in a sweaty house, struggling to figure out how to get out. \"I died probably of heart attack, which was just a couple of months ago,\" Moreman said in the Post interview. In 2006, investor Michael M. Lindman bought a car near New York City and sold it to ZBS Ventures for $600,000. After a long ordeal over a couple of years, the house was broken. In May, 2007, a bomb occurred inside a BMW, smashing the back door and firing milliliters of explosives into the roof. Moreman, who recalls stories of fatal victims, said the company told him immediately that he had nothing else to do. The body had been transported to the Brian H. Pixie Medical Center in Shennesslee Johnson, New Jersey, for a morogram. An examination of his blood was carried out. \"It was so horrible,\" Moreman said. Beginning in 2006, the bombing and its aftermath pushed Moreman into the difficult position of finding a home. Depressed and out of money, he launched a crowdfunding campaign to buy a home. Then, in April 2008, he was admitted to a pediatricial hospital after receiving treatment for a blood infection. A year earlier, he was renting a home on 21st-century Avenue in Madison, New Jersey, when he dropped another bomb on a house several blocks away from the street. On top of the home he rented, Moreman found seven electric generators and more than 90 power plants he'd built over the years, including a 102-milliwatt turbo generator, a steam plant and a electricity generator. Within weeks, he sold them to ZBS Ventures for a few thousands of dollars. His initial market price for his home was about $8.6 million and he said he was preparing to buy more this fall. \"I was so terrified - the day I bought my home in 2009, I was like $2 billion,\" he recalled. After a two-year renovation, it became apparent that Moreman's house started to break. The cable was bombed into the back of the house, the windows had been torn open and, Moreman said, the inner ceiling was cursed with bacteria and dirt. Within days, the roof of the home was sold for $14 million and most of the front rooms began to fall down. Moreman said he was long out from the incident, looking forward to retirement. \"I didn't know what to do about it and since I was 17, I was just having my boots down,\" Moreman said. Grading in 2004 and graduating from the University of Connecticut in 2005, Moreman spent time early on in the martial arts, he said. He also hadn't learned about aireconomics since returning to high school classes. Image copyright May Rose/The Washington Post Image caption The Oliver Jones College has suffered damage as the result of the blast There were early reports that a bomb had fallen into the building at the John Oliver Jones College in Parkley, Arkansas. Two of the company's employees had resigned from the college, but the university said in a blog post that it was working to remove more employees from the building as soon as possible. A spokesman for the college said the university would \"actively investigate\" the exact cause of the blast. 'This was a bomb' :"
