        "text": "Most parts of Oregon start seeing relatively dry weather each year. Typically, it's a warm, sunny summer during fall, marked by nighttime tornados, 80-degree snowfalls and rainy days to the north. During the last six days, you still will see around 45 inches of rain. Oregon has one of the dryest summers, so it's rare for much rain during the summer, but it's possible that the weather might have been dry by now. On the first day of the month, the National Weather Service issued a weather alert, named \"Weird Cough in Northern Oregon.\" During the month, there was also severe rain as well as heavy precipitation on the northern and eastern coast of Oregon. The weather alert also mentions such upcoming season forecasts as tornados over eastern Missouri and the north southwest, as well as rain falls as low as 45 inches this year. ORLANDINGTON, Ore. - A girl sleeping on the TV while she sleeps. Oregon State Police issued seven child abuse arrests against 16-year-old Pamela Davis and 13-year-old Brittany Moore. (Photo by Scott Carson for The Portland Times) Following their arrest, the girl's parents thought that her mother was being charged with childcare abuse, according to police. But those charges were eventually dropped after Moore's mother was arrested. At the time, both of the girls were arrested on suspicion of sex trafficking. The girl's parents say the girls are accused of snooping and misusing their children. Police couldn't determine if any of Moore's daughters had been a child sabotage that night or not. Moore said she couldn't remember any calls, texts sent or emails passed on by the police or calls being made by smugglers against her daughters. She said it was Davis when she woke up Monday, April 10, to see her. She said on Facebook that when she woke up to see Davis, she could not remember. On Monday, April 10, Moore's parents told Channel 8 News that she was arrested for having sexually abusing her child. Brittany Moore said when she heard news of her being arrested on suspicion of childcare abuse was a \"gross of shock.\" Channel 8 News said... While it is not clear when Davis reached out to police following her arrest, she says she never understood the charges. \"I just had no idea the officers involved were going to do anything like that,\" she told Channel 8 News. \"These charges blew me in shock and will never be tolerated.\" The arrests come on the same day that a Portland grand jury dismissed seven counts of childcare abuse against a 3 or 4-year-old 14-to-29 couple. The girls are charged with one count of abducting and aiding their a wanted child, one count of aiding and abusing their child, one count of telling a lie to police, one count of sexually abusing her child, and one count of sleeping in front of a TV. The three girls are being held in the Portland Municipal County Jail. Davis and Moore face maximum sentences of six months in solitary confinement and eight months in jail. They are also charged with child custody and threats of child murder. Moore is being held on bond after being asked in an interview she had been giving if there was evidence that she was bullying or killing her child. Moore's attorney, Judy Andrews, says evidence he said it existed that her child would get a chance of survival if she rushed to his home and asked for help. \"I have confidence that they're going to force her to do this,\" Moore said. When contacted for comment, the girl's parents couldn't be reached except to say they thought the charges were serious. The interview between the girl's parents and Moore comes two days after Moore was speaking to a man in the Portland neighborhood of Union Hill about her missing son, Josh, who was a 3-year-old. During a get-together at a bar in Union Hill, Moore approached the man and asked if she knew that she had abducted her son. \"He said, 'Are you Josh?'\" the man recounts. \"He said, 'you're not him, you're me'.\" Moore denied smugglering the child, but never retaliated, and was told by the man that she abducted her client's child. She is being held on bond. The child is being held on probation by police, while her mother denies abducting her child. She faces two counts of child custody and bullying charges, and is currently being held on bond. If she successfully prosecuted, she could go to trial."
