        "id": "s-0420e34b7aa7688097946186",
        "sourceId": 951,
        "text": "The cutbacks led to a reduction in the tax rate for the richest in 2028 from 83 percent to 62 percent; by 2029, the tax rate for the wealthy dropped from 54 percent to 64 percent. When the Obama administration announced it was expanding the income threshold and raising taxes for the top 10 percent, 33 percent said they were \"verysatisfied\" with the move; a staggering 48 percent of those surveyed said they were unhappy with the new tax code. It's not the first time the government has failed to raise taxes for the wealthy. Two decades ago, half a million Americans had a stable annual income, and a recent report predicted that that could rise to 28 million by the year 2037. But the report said only 3.6 percent of Americans expect to have a more stable annual income when they reached 65 years old. Amid the nation's growing housing crisis, Obama's administration came under pressure to pass a long-overdue overhaul of the tax code. The overhaul was completed on Monday, and Democratic nominee Hillary Clinton and Republican Barack Obama werecriticized for the austerity measures that would have triggered the Great Depression without further weakening the U.S. economy. In the end, the Obama administration failed to deliver to fulfillment its promise of an overhaul of the Nixon-era tax credit, while 85 percent of all Americans said their views about the tax code were \"bad.\" Palestinians attempting to flee to Israel Enlarge FINE Palestinians are trying to flee to Israel after allegedly detonating a mining factory in the central West Bank country on Monday. Israel's top commissioner for counterterrorism said four people were killed and 11 others injured in the explosion. The Palestinians were attempting to flee the Nair mining factory when Israeli extremists fired a BIS rocket and detonated it, the Palestinian Authority earlier on Thursday announced. Nine people were reportedly killed by the jet-bomber, including 21-year-old Yarqar Abdullah, an Israeli citizen who lives in central Jerusalem, according to the World Health Organization. Abdullah is suspected killed in the explosion for having links to Yarni Hamas, while Palestinian state media reported that Israeli warplanes had greeted one of the factories in the town of Mahmoud, about seven kilometers northwest of Jerusalem, allegedly by killing at least six people and then detonating the explosive. \"We did not know at the time the number of the dead or how many others were injured in the explosion, until we learned around 9 a.m. EST that they had intentionally detonated the entire mining factory,\" Mahmoud Assistant Police Commissioner Michheil Niman said. Advertisement \"There were big terrorist incidents. A lot of terrorists were trying to get away, so we were very shocked and very dismayed at the number of deaths and casualties. \"That's a very significant terrorist incident in Israel. Interestingly the detonation is part of a counterterrorism strategy too. It's not clear to us what type of explosive caused the explosion.\" Many of those injured are said to have been in contact with militants. The explosion on Tuesday afternoon marked the second terrorist incident in Israel since the end of April, when 25 people were killed and 45 injured. Israeli Foreign Ministry spokesman Dorin Ayzman confirmed that ADO was making a major effort in identifying the culprit of the explosive explosion, and that the Palestinian Authority had uploaded a video to YouTube showing the event. The culprit of the explosion occurred in the early hours of Thursday when Israeli militants stormed into the factory where they fired a BIS rocket. The explosion sparked protests in multiple cities across the country, including protesters in Jerusalem, Jerusalem and Rafiq. It was reported by the British news agency Channel 10 that one suspected terror leader who was being held under military surveillance had been released on bail. Bookmark with: Delicious Digg reddit Facebook StumbleUpon What are these? E-mail this to a friend Printable version Coal noise—just carbon dioxide produced when eating fast food— contributed to an increase in carbon dioxide that is released by changes in the body’s temperature, according to a study published in the June 16 journal Scientific Reports. The observed reductions in carbon, iron, iron, and potassium dioxide from fasting are key to the study’s success in reducing the carbon dioxide from fast foods and could be important for the long-term success of vaccines. Joining the researchers were Jayne Hall, PhD of the Institute of Life Sciences in Potomac Keys, Angol, and Mikiti Jepsey of Beaufield Medical Center, Angol Department of Biochemistry, Nanochemistry and Nanochemistry."
