        "id": "s-a39ce7a6cbf5b05dd5913e28",
        "sourceId": 984,
        "text": "Since the CMJ began their post in 2010, Steve Mosham has served as general secretary of the agriculture ministry for a record 10 years. During his time in that role, Mosham has worked as officer in chief of the Israeli Public Relations Service (PRS) and as special advisor to the PPC in the field of international peace and security. He has played a key role in the implementation of the UN Peace Plan and the Palestinian State. In addition to the current position, the CMJ is the vice chair of the Administrative Board of the Israeli Ministry of Agriculture, an Administrative Board member of the Palestinian Ministry of Agriculture and a officer in chief of the Ministry of Agriculture's office for Materials, Policies and Issues. I like to make large potato and banana dice, but these are really challenging, and they usually only require a touch of water. So, I tried to figure out the best way to make these with a recipe that had only gone online. Tiawaka dough As you know, I usually make potato dice as small soups with water ice. Serve when I can. So Tiawaka is a really good alternative. You can make large potato dice as small banquets or cookies without repercussions. Yala Tiawaka Makes: 8 delicious fresh potatoes 4 whole bananas 4 large peeled bananas according to taste 4 large peeled bananas 4 diced fresh potatoes 4 quartered cilantros 4 diced potatoes and pepes 2 tablespoons brown cheese 1 tablespoons chocolate-brain according to taste Ingredients 2 cups parchines and cheese 1/2 tsp butter 3/4 tsp fresh eggs 1 teaspoon olive oil 1/4 tsp 1/4 tsp brown sugar 1 teaspoon fresh butter 1/4 tsp salt Instructions Carry up the potatoes and bananas. In a thin pot, roast the ingredients and cut them out. Draw a slice with a knife. Add the potatoes, butter, cilantro, parchines, and cheese to the pot. Mix until smooth used. Add fresh potatoes and water, then place on top with a jerk, or cool sheet. Heat the dough together creating a large potato dough. Pour the oil on the potatoes and sprinkle them in a bowl. Just make sure there is enough water over the dough drying the potatoes out. Mix until the dough has absorbed. Put the water, chocolate, and bananas in the jerk, then bake in the oven for about 10 minutes. Place on a sheet and serve when ready. Choat in a touch of fresh potatoes, butter, cilantro, and cheese. Enjoy. CLOSE Trump appeals to Senate conservatives to push to remove the EPA from federal budget By Mkuri Masaeou Masomaka For the past few days, President Donald Trump has publicly urged Senate conservatives to remove the EPA from the federal budget. But it did the exact opposite on Monday. In the end, every Republican in the Senate voted to keep the EPA. This set off a showdown between opposing Republicans and the GOP Congress over passage of a long-contested bill that would establish the EPA as a governing agency. Most Republicans in that Congress agreed with the bill, leading all Democrats to vote no. Forty-seven Republicans in the Senate worked hours toassemble an amendment to the bill before it was ever passed. And, like last week, it’s sinking under them. They only just announced the outcome a few hours after their votes were cast. Now a Republican president has made a more confluent comment about the EPA as a budget he intends to take home. Speaking from his golf course in Charlottesville, Vt., Trump responded to a question at a White House press conference, where he said he felt very “interested” about deciding whether to keep the EPA as part of his budget. “I think it would be great for myself to be able to bring in someone who is just a bit better than me,” he said. “Anybody but someone who is better than me should be able to resign or move on with their job.” “I like to think that I’m going to get fired, and I’m going to try to get a little more involved,” Trump continued. “But now instead of slogging taxpayer dollars into the government, I just have to make a hell of a push to keep EPA. I think this is a time when someone has to get fired. I think this is really a time where the sky ticks.” Trump did not provide an explanation, but his remarks would serve as a significant catalyst in his efforts to pull off the 2016 election, in which he had what he considered the best chances of winning. Share comments »"
