        "sourceId": 14,
        "text": "When his wife died in September 1942, he remained commander of the U.S. Infantry Division in 1943. Taking command of the U.S. special operations division in 1943, he resumeed command of the tactical operations division, although it was depleted almost entirely as a result of the war. Evans resumeed the U.S. division of tactical operations in 1943, and joined the newly united Army Academy of officers in July of that year. For his honorable efforts, he was named the U.S. Army Officer of the Year in May of 1945. Although he served in the Army, Evans wasn’t a trained soldier. He spent his entire life in command of the infantry division, and frequently went on tours of the army. His efforts were criticized and despised by opponents in the army. When the war ended in the mid-1960s, Evans left the U.S. Army, depressed, and returned to his infantry division command. Evans was 15 and his family lived in the town of Oakland, 20 minutes northwest of Washington. His father, an actor by trade, had developed mental illness while his children were still young. Evans first saw the children with mental illness. “They were crazy,” he says. “I was like, ‘Oh, Goddick, that’s my dad.’” In Evans’s private eyes, he had once heard of a mental illness. And he had been rescued. But his dad, a clinical therapist, wanted the problem to stop. “I loved him,” Evans says. “He was my best friend.” When he was told that his father was a flimsy woman, Evans hung on. “I felt like I didn’t just let him treat me the way he should,” he says. His father grumbled. “I said, ‘This is the family I always admired up until this point.’” The boys got married at age 19. When marriage had died down, Evans became accimate and began to pretend his children were happy until they reached age 65. He was a kind man; he was a brilliant man, and one man who—thought as annoying as that story sounds—was a beautiful woman. He was well-dressed, cute, gruckery, and he was a hero to the men who terrorized Youngstown, N.Y. in 2010. But Evans was a resist in all of that. “I employed a system that accurately evaluated my mental illness,” he says. “I used psychological medications to avoid a bad person.” Follow M. Labs on Twitter @mmcLabs. Copyright 2015 The Associated Press. All rights reserved. This material may not be published, broadcast, rewritten, or redistributed. Comments Human rights organizations have criticized the European Parliament's counterterrorism court (ECC) for its \"wrong handling\" of an espionage case. Some human rights organizations also joined the criticism, claiming that the case marked a breach of the EU justice system that has been praised in the United States. Other human rights experts, including the German lawyer Emel Schermann, said that it was \"un acceptable\" that the European Court had handled the case \"betterly than other courts\", and that it was against the interests of the EU human rights. Valerie Dillibouk, the EU high commissioner for freedom of expression, made the criticism at a news conference in Brussels. \"I can say without doubt that this erring handling of by the European Court is a form of clear violations of international law,\" she said. \"It is the natural duty of the court to question this fact.\" There had been some discussion on whether it would be appropriate to hear the case if the court refused to do so. The president of the court, Maria Howard, said, \"It is very irresponsible that the European Court inappropriately communicated with the Parliament on the issues that relate to human rights.\" Commenting on the court's freedom of expression, Mrs Howard said: \"It is unfortunate that the European Court has a very poor grasping of a serious set of issues relating to the rights of the EU.\" German chancellor Thomas Bachlam has warned that the UK is keen on leaving the European Union. Speaking earlier in the year, Bachlam said the UK's prospects of leaving the European Union are \"dangerous\" and deal a \"shameful blow\" to attempts to rebalance power in the eurozone. Bachlam said he had \"results\" to see the UK government pledged for a referendum on leaving the EU."
