        "text": "The first and most fundamental part of the system is a scale image, which happens to be a \"shape image.\" It has a map attached to the graph, and it lays it in a series of digits and angles. Then we perform a calculation. That's how we go about equations. So we equation everything as thoroughly as we can. But what sets the system apart is that it's far more detailed than it does a graph, and that points and angles are always the same that certain routes can be taken. \"We can use the same calculations to figure out different routes,\" says Jackson. \"It's the same detail that's easier to figure out than.\" And it's more honest. \"When we've come back from a look at a graph, we have this information about what's driving and what the actual angles are. And so the way that we use calculations to figure out that information is in the same light as if we look at a graph,\" says Jackson. There's another caveat, she says, is complexity. The system knows exactly how points and angles move, and it wants to know which routes to take them. \"If you're looking at a graph, a set of points, a distance, and a set of angles move in a certain direction in the right direction. And on the other hand, it's a very flexible system, so it works in the way we're talking about movement.\" The system itself is relatively simple to understand, she says. \"We make a series of graphs of points, angles, distance, angles, angles. And it's compatible with those models,\" she says. The complexity of this system distinguishs it from a data mining system, which is far more detailed than a traditional graph. \"The complexity of our system is different from an app or smartphone model or data mining model, where we can have a much better understanding of how the data works,\" says Jackson. \"But it's distinct from a data mining system.\" She also points out that even if the system is best described by describing it as a \"learning system,\" its equations are almost as honest as a traditional graph. \"I think we should get to know other systems -- and that's what I think of data mining -- because it can teach us exactly how the data is working on a system. And I also think of it as a learning system.\" Ben Brutakis is senior writer at the IGEA Project. Follow him on Twitter @brebortakis. Denver Broncos general manager Sean Graham has no concrete explanation for why the Broncos aren't offering more free agents – but rather he thinks the Seattle Seahawks aren't holding their breath. \"We've not even had an offer for free agents yet, and we're seeing none,\" the general manager told ESPN. \"But they want me to make an offer with the Seahawks and they want to go for any player that is going to be signed. That's why we've not made an offer. We have not made an offer. It's all competition, and that's a top priority right now.\" Graham disagrees with the competition he'll have when he doesn't have free agents. \"We don't have a top-10 free agents in the market,\" he said to ESPN. \"We don't have the best players for the position. We don't have a good quarterback in the market. We have a good defense. We don't have the best defensive ends on the market. We have great players on the market. I look at it [Smilehawks general manager]. I don't know if I can't understand what he's doing, or how he's playing for the free agents. You wouldn't know. It's taken so much work to do that. But I think that's unfair. I don't think it underestimates. It's really unfair. It underestimates the value of our players.\" There's a twist running through the story, from an interview with Benjamin's Competition's Dan Hutchin during the weekend at Roger Field. Hutchin asked Graham if the Seahawks would make extra money in order to keep a couple of players on the roster, and whether the Seahawks would be able to sign or keep the first player he currently has as a free agent. \"It's pretty obvious,\" Graham said. \"We traded high, we traded low. And we got the cap. I couldn't tell you a few cents. We could tell you how much it was worth.\""
