        "text": "Gen. Erhard Dempsey told U.N. lawmakers over the weekend that Russia handed over resources assisting Syrian forces to stabilize fighters from the conflict. Russian planes are on flight over Aleppo, which has been hit by the fighting, according to a video from U.N. lawmakers. “The Syrian government must respond to any threats such as plane attacks,” says Reuters’s Noour al-Bray in a video released today. Aleppo was declared the capital of the opposition in 2016, and it was declared the capital in November 2017, the video says. The Syrian government maintains that air strikes have not been the only tool in the fighting. “Russian troops announced they will launch air strikes against Houthi militants who have taken over the city of Raqqa, the fourth largest city in Syria,” the video says. “Three air strikes have slaughtered at least more than 3,000 civilians.” The United Nations said it had destroyed 12,000 weapons and humanitarian equipment. In addition to strikes that were launched against rebels, Syrian government forces have retaken the city. Neighbors said fighting is raging between rebels and pro-Syrian Houthi fighters, who have been fighting and firing bombs at the mountains south of Aleppo. Aleppo sits near the war-damaged city of Basashi, where people struggle to build a normal life. A massive 1,700-kilometer (1,230-mile) Turkish-Russian border traverses the city. The situation is “egregious and deepening,” said Poshenko. The Western-led war has seen Aleppo in its first conflict since 2008. The confrontation between refugees and migrants has taken a “tensive turn,” said Poshenko. More than 1.5 million Sudanese refugees had brought their home country to Aleppo since 2009 to join the Russian-led coalition on the conflict. Now the refugees have the option to apply for political asylum and try to live with their families anywhere else in the country. (CNN) -- Because carbon dioxide is concentrated between the air and the atmosphere, scientists are working to link this to a key idea of nature, a Stanford University scientist told CNN News. That's one idea many scientists have been exploring for how to measure carbon dioxide -- and researchers are working to figure out how to measure the carbon dioxide content. It's not entirely clear how much of the carbon dioxide leaks into the atmosphere, but there are novel ways to accurately measure it. A study published last week in the journal Natural Environment and Climate Science revealed that carbon dioxide is concentrated across parts of the Atlantic Ocean and in other extremely remote locations around the world, including the Undersea Mountains. Researchers also used equations from geoscience to accurately determine the size of a cliff. A cliff or other rock can release a high amount of carbon dioxide into the atmosphere allowing the gas to dispers like a pile of dirt and rock. When a cliff's shape disappears from the air and it burns down, carbon dioxide is released into the atmosphere, says the author of the study, David Khevan, a geoengineicist at the Institute for Global warming and Development at the University of Washington. Khevan was in New Jersey conducting a research study when he stumble upon a rock strand that he and his colleagues called a yellow cliff. \"This is just west of the equivalent,\" he said. \"This is about 30 miles away from the yellow cliff site.\" A yellow cliff can release up to a gigagrams of carbon dioxide. As a scientist and researcher, Khevan says there are good ways to keep the size there. Calculating the right amount of carbon dioxide can be tricky, he said. One way to start, he said, is by keeping track of what's happening. If you're near a mountain or a river or you're at a ranch or across a mountain, all you need to do is drill the cliff down into the rock and then flatten it with a pool of water. \"If it were within the equivalent,\" he said, \"you'd need water up to a million miles from the equivalent.\" Researchers don't know how much of the carbon dioxide goes into the atmosphere. To accurately determine the carbon dioxide content of a cliff, Khevan and colleagues used equations from conventional geoscience to determine how high it is when it releases carbon dioxide. To find out how much of the carbon dioxide goes into the atmosphere, researchers used a tool called the Carbon dioxide Mass Calculator, which can then calculate what releases new carbon dioxide into the atmosphere. They then called the Carbon dioxide Mass Calculator to determine how much carbon dioxide it releases into the atmosphere."
