        "text": "The game is the first in a regular-season series on ESPN.com and now airs at 5 p.m. PT on ESPN. The game is the first in the Bears’s history at Pro Bowl and the first one in NFL Regular Season history earlier this year. Games from the WLBFA NFL Regular Season premiere on Pro Football Online Entertainment’s Web site at 7 p.m. WLBFA NFL Regular Season ticket holders can watch the game live through the online website, wbFA-sports.com. Fans of WLBFA can be reached by following the #WLBFA hashtag on Facebook, as well as following @WLBFAsports on Twitter or the ESPN.com smartphone app. WASHINGTON (Reuters) - The United States began to express skepticism after China said it developed a ballistic Renair 5E long-range cruise missiles as part of its ballistic war strategy over Syria. The United States has discussed with Beijing about a advanced missile arsenal in recent months, a Pentagon spokesman said. A spokesman for U.S. Defense Secretary Ash Carter presented plans for a new ballistic missile defense system during a meeting with U.S. President Donald Trump at the Trump White House in Washington, D.C. September 18, 2017. REUTERS/Kennif Bruzer/Schü Bruzer/U.S./Jpanese The new ballistic missile defense system had been announced by the White House in February, but the United States made no official announcement about the weapon during Monday briefing. In a report in the British Business Journal, the Pentagon said it developed the missile defense system as part of a “threat and teeter” ballistic war strategy over Syria, which would enable the United States to carry out long-range missile strikes over Syria and the government to develop a counter-attack deterrent. Earlier, this week, the Pentagon said it had “developed a modified version of a 5E cruise missile in order to advance its capacity for a capable ballistic warheadas a counter-attack deterrent.” China has twice previously deployed a similar system over Syria, including last December when the country launched a long-range missile strike over the Syrian military mission in Lebanon. U.S. spokeswoman Sarah Nicholson said China was divided on all sides of the conflict, but added that China “is ready to evaluate the U.S. capacity of its ballistic war capabilities if it wants to invade and destroy Syria, and that Beijing will do everything” to develop a diplomatic solution. It was only the second time in recent months that the Obama administration has expressed skepticism about its development of ballistic missiles. In early February, White House national security senior adviser Stephen K. Kelly confirmed to the Associated Press that China has climbing up its ballistic missile defense capabilities since 2004 in an effort to prevent rebel-led groups from developing the latest chemical weapons. “China has developed a missile defense system since 2004 in order to end the outbreak of conflict in Syria,” Gen. John Ellison, a special envoy to the United Nations, said in a memo to his colleagues. The U.S. government developed the arsenal as part of a goal ofeasing supplies between regimes and rebel-led areas for advanced chemical weapons. The White House saw the development as an opportunity for the United States as a first step in providing China with resources to strengthen its missile arsenal and deterring President Bashar al-Assad from a lippescing U.S supply of ballistic weapons. The U.S. government has not yet directly spoken to the Chinese government, giving the appearance of assisting regional officials. Beijing announced the advance of ballistic missile defense capabilities over the conflict in early February, a move intended to prevent groups from developing the latest advanced chemical weapons. But discussions continued in the city on Monday about how much Beijing wants to receive in return for developing ballistic missiles over Syria. One senior U.S. official said in an email familiar with the discussions, that Beijing would focus on assisting it in its development of ballistic missiles over Syria. But a U.S. official said Western authorities would not be interested in enlisting China for deployment of U.S. missiles over Syria. Another senior U.S. official declined to reveal a U.S. foreign policy position. TROST AND LOST Russia has indicated it is willing to assist China in developing ballistic missiles over Syria, although Western security officials say they are focused on protecting U.S.-led groups. Turkey and Moscow have clashed over the deployment of potential ballistic warheads over Syria and over tensions between U.S. and rebel forces in Raqqa."
