        "id": "s-82662631e36a65e65bf9cfcf",
        "sourceId": 897,
        "text": "\"In accordance with all previously passed and applied provisions of the Defense Authorization Act, the Pentagon's obligations to protect employees from unauthorized discrimination or discrimination of a religion or race remain to be as defined by the Executive Order,\" the Pentagon said in its statement. \"Though the Pentagon's Executive Order addresses national antitrust laws and prohibits private employees from discriminating on the basis of sexual orientation, there remains a clear responsibility on the Pentagon to address all forms of discrimination, act to ensure companies and contractors have adequate resources to fight discrimination on the basis of sexual orientation, gender identity or race in the private sector, and, more specifically, prevent any private employee from discriminating on the basis of sexual orientation.\" As to the judge's reaction, Edward Fallon, a Republican radio host, said on \"This Week\" with Rush Limbaugh that the Pentagon's Executive Order, which aims to protect federal employees from workplace discrimination, did not comply with all of the law's requirements. \"I'm not trying to misrepresent the essence of the Executive Order,\" he said. \"I find this [exele Order] very offensive and very revealing. It's not even just politically correct, it represents a very clear violation of the Fourth Amendment's protections. The Pentagon's whole reaction to this is simply disturbing.\" One of the most outspoken critics of Trump's decision is Rep. Joe Corocki (R-FIN), who said he believes the judge's ruling equates directly to the GOP's support for a ban on same sex marriage. \"While the Supreme Court's ruling raise important questions, it raises questions about how the Pentagon will proceed in implementing its protections and whether the Pentagon will take a leading role in implementing these rules,\" he said in a statement. The Executive Order also prohibits federal employers from threatening to fire or otherwise detain employees on the basis of sexual orientation, but the Pentagon's attorney general, Col. Michael Boris, didn't say which part of the rule it applies. The judge's reaction comes amid growing speculation that Trump's administration would phase out all transgender service members on the basis of sexual orientation or race. Human rights groups also spoke out against Trump's decision, saying the Defense Department would take a different approach to the judge's ruling. \"The Executive Order goes to the beyond of saying 'One person is trans and another person is gay,'\" said Chelsea Kalvevan, president of the Human Rights Campaign. \"We're not looking to change that, but we're in some ways interested in making the progress we can in enforcement the law.\" The Human Rights Working Group, which is representing Flynn, who left in May to serve as the Pentagon's deputy attorney general, said he regrets the judge and won't support protections for LGBT people and other minority groups. One month after the Executive Order protected transgender service members and other federal employees, President George W. Bush publicly said in a statement late Friday that the Pentagon's Executive Order was a significant step in increasing protections for transgender troops. \"The leaders of our nation's military must realize that these rules cannot be obscured in the face of a shifting Pentagon policy. The Executive Order makes it impossible for a military officer, contractor, or another member of the government to qualify for these protections,\" Bush said in a statement. \"All U.S. troops, including LGBT people, should be protected under the same conditions provided by the Executive Order.\" A spokesperson for the attorney general's office was not immediately available, saying the policy might change in light of the judge's decision. \"In addition to ruling on this issue, the Defense Department will not comply with the judge,\" White House Press Secretary Charlie Awane said. \"The Defense Department will not comment on this.\" The Pentagon will continue to rule on protections for transgender, bisexual and transgender people serving in the US military, he said. \"The Trump administration will apply to the Executive Order to any existing categories of discrimination, such as bisexual, transgender and transgender, or sexual orientation,\" an attorney for the Human Rights Campaign said in a statement. \"This order protects all military personnel from discrimination, nondiscrimination, and any other discrimination on the basis of sexual orientation, race, gender or other sexual orientation. We are saddened by the Supreme Court's ruling on LGBT people, LGBT people, and other minority groups,\" Kalvevan said. Information from The Associated Press contributed to this report. Alex Murray's game-winning home goal marks his first goal for the New York Sounders since the team's 2-1 win at Atlanta FC (April 2nd)."
